  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/assets/19e9763d62ca7818024bf49522a127a48dd04f76d1175c93e060a1043b6a5795.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={f}^{\u2033}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?",
  "worked_solution": "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${f}^{\u2033}(x)$ must switch sign. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${f}^{\u2033}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={f}^{\u2033}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=0$ and $x=4.$ \n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=0$ and $x=4.$",
